  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A pipette is calibrated to deliver one fixed volume, usually 25.0 cm³, to within 0.05 cm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A burette has a tap and a graduated scale, so it can deliver any volume drop by drop.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Water wets glass, so the meniscus curves down and the lowest point is the volume; reading at eye level avoids parallax.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The graduations are at 0.1 cm³, and each is read to half a division: 0.05 cm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It has a single mark, and the solution is made up to that mark with solvent.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The dilution is very exothermic; adding acid to water keeps the mixture dilute and prevents spitting.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Wafting brings a small amount of vapour to the nose; putting the nose to the tube can cause injury.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zn²⁺ gives a white precipitate soluble in both reagents; Al³⁺ is soluble in NaOH but not in excess ammonia.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Copper(II) hydroxide is pale blue and dissolves in excess ammonia to give the deep blue tetraamminecopper(II) ion.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Iron(II) hydroxide is green; iron(III) hydroxide is red-brown.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ammonium salts release ammonia with an alkali; the gas turns damp red litmus blue.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sodium gives a strong golden yellow flame, which is why sodium street lamps are yellow.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Potassium gives a lilac flame; sodium's yellow can mask it, so a cobalt glass may be used.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Calcium gives a brick-red flame; lithium gives crimson and barium apple-green.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Oxygen supports combustion, so it makes a glowing splint burst into flame.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen ignites with a characteristic pop when a lighted splint is applied.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Carbon dioxide forms insoluble calcium carbonate, which makes the limewater milky.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ammonia is the only common alkaline gas: it dissolves in the water on the paper and turns litmus blue.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Chlorine forms hypochlorous acid with water, which bleaches the indicator.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Barium sulphate is a white precipitate that is insoluble in dilute acid; the acid removes carbonate and sulphite interference.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Silver chloride is white and dissolves in dilute ammonia; the bromide is cream and needs concentrated ammonia, and the iodide is yellow and insoluble.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Silver bromide is the cream precipitate soluble only in concentrated ammonia.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Silver iodide is yellow and does not dissolve in ammonia at all.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A carbonate gives carbon dioxide with an acid, and that gas turns limewater milky.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The solid is trapped by the filter paper while the liquid passes through as the filtrate.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The more volatile liquid distils over first, and the fractions are collected separately.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The pigments move different distances up the paper because they have different solubilities.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rf is the ratio of the distance the substance travelled to the distance the solvent travelled, so it is between 0 and 1.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rf = 4.0/8.0 = 0.50.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A pure substance has a sharp melting point; an impurity lowers it and spreads it over a range.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Dissolving in hot solvent and cooling slowly leaves the impurity behind in the mother liquor.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The yield is always below 100 %, because some product is always lost in handling.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    16.0/20.0 × 100 = 80 %.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    0.05/25.00 × 100 = 0.2 %.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Either works for a strong acid–strong base titration, because the pH changes sharply over a wide range.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The end point is what you observe; the equivalence point is what the equation predicts, and they are close but not identical.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Concentrated sulphuric acid absorbs water vapour and can be used to dry most gases, though not ammonia.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An acid and a base react; calcium oxide is used to dry ammonia instead.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A volumetric flask is calibrated to a single mark and is the most accurate of the four for one fixed volume.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The third titre is not concordant and is discarded; the mean of the first two is 20.125, quoted as 20.13 cm³.
  ]

]

